view-if: anti_socialist_coalition_r >= 50 and pnf_seating_disp > ul_seating_disp + ldr_seating_disp and (prime_minister_party != "PNF" or prime_minister = "D'Annunzio")
go-to: dannunzio_power if prime_minister = "D'Annunzio"

It seems our <span style="color: [+ communist_colour +];">radicalism</span> and abstentionism has pushed the LDR and UL to ally with the PNF, as a junior partner to their list. [+ pnf_leader +] has made himself crucial to this coalition, and been able to come out on top in the inter-party struggle, being appointed Prime Minister by the <span style="color: [+ i_colour +];">**King**</span>.

- @pnf_prime_minister: What now?

@dannunzio_power

The PNF, under the leadership of Gabriele D'Annunzio, is able to form a coalition, keeping himself as Prime Minister in a majority coalition.

What now?

- @civil_war: We cannot wait any longer. Mobilise the Red Guard!
- @nothing_dannunzio: Bide our time further.

@nothing_dannunzio
subtitle: This will end the game.

[+ pnf_leader +] acts fast, consolidating <span style="color: #008C45;">**It**</span><span style="color: #E5E4E2;">**a**</span><span style="color: #CD212A;">**ly**</span> into a corporative state and empowering the PNF and D'Annunzio into effective dictators of <span style="color: #008C45;">**It**</span><span style="color: #E5E4E2;">**a**</span><span style="color: #CD212A;">**ly**</span> for the foresseable future.

<span style="color: #008C45;">**It**</span><span style="color: #E5E4E2;">**a**</span><span style="color: #CD212A;">**ly**</span> is lost.

- @game_over: Game over.

@grand_coalition_yes
subtitle: [? if participating = 1 : This is extremely unpopular with the Reformist faction. ?]
on-arrival: {!
if (Q.participating == 1) {
    Q.reformist_dissent += 15;
}
Q.psi_in_government = 0; 
if (Q.year == 1919) {
    Q.prime_minister = "Nitti"; 
    Q.prime_minister_party = Q.ldr_name;
} 
if (Q.year == 1920 || Q.year == 1921) {
    Q.prime_minister = "Giolitti";
    Q.prime_minister_party = Q.ul_name;
}
if (Q.year == 1921) {
    Q.prime_minister = "Bonomi";
    Q.prime_minister_party = "PSRI";
}
if (Q.year > 1921) {
    Q.prime_minister = "Orlando";
    Q.prime_minister_party = Q.ldr_name;
}
Q.interior_minister_party = Q.ds_name; 
Q.foreign_minister_party = "UL"; 
Q.justice_minister_party = "I"; 
Q.treasury_minister_party = "LDR"; 
Q.finance_minister_party = "LDR"; 
Q.public_works_minister_party = Q.ds_name; 
Q.public_education_minister_party = "LDR"; 
Q.agriculture_minister_party = "LDR"; 
Q.labour_minister_party = "UL";
!}

Though tense negotiations between the <span style="color: [+ ldr_colour +];">Liberals</span> and newly formed <span style="color: [+ ppi_colour +];">Catholic</span> parties, the coalition of the PPI, [+ ldr_name +], [+ ul_name +] and [+ ds_name +] is established.

@turati
title: Filippo Turati for Prime Minister
subtitle: Turati leads the Reformist wing, and is an acceptable candidate for Prime Minister.
choose-if: reformists_defect = 0
unavailable-subtitle: Turati is no longer a member of the [+ psi_name +].
on-arrival: psi_in_government = 1; prime_minister = "Turati"; prime_minister_party = psi_name;
go-to: ministries

Filippo Turati's appointment as Prime Minister is approved by all our potential allies.

@bissolati
title: Leonida Bissolati for Prime Minister
view-if: not bissolati_dead
subtitle: Bissolati is a prominent leader of the PSRI, formerly affiliated with the [+ psi_name +].
on-arrival: psi_in_government = 1; prime_minister = "Bissolati"; prime_minister_party = "PSRI"; leverage += 5; maximalist_dissent += 2; communist_dissent += 5;
go-to: ministries

This is highly unexpected, but Bissolati's PSRI roots, as well as his commitment to <span style="color: #9B0000;">socialism</span>, leaves little grumbling from our party.

@bonomi
title: Ivanoe Bonomi for Prime Minister
view-if: bissolati_dead
subtitle: Bonomi is a prominent leader of the PSRI, formerly affiliated with the [+ psi_name +].
on-arrival: psi_in_government = 1; prime_minister = "Bonomi"; prime_minister_party = "PSRI"; leverage += 5; maximalist_dissent += 4; communist_dissent += 7;
go-to: ministries

This is highly unexpected, but Bonomi's PSRI roots leaves merely some grumbling from our party.

@orlando
title: Let the [+ ldr_name +] have their Prime Minister. This will improve our leverage.
subtitle: Vittorio Emanuele Orlando will be reappointed Prime Minister. This is unexpected and will cause internal dissent, but may give us a better position in negotiating for other cabinet positions.
on-arrival: reformist_dissent += 5; maximalist_dissent += 5; communist_dissent += 10; ldr_relation += 10; ds_relation += 5; prime_minister = "Orlando"; prime_minister_party = ldr_name; psi_in_government = 1; leverage += 10
go-to: ministries

@giolitti
title: Let the [+ ul_name +] have their Prime Minister. This will improve our leverage.
subtitle: Giovanni Giolitti will be reappointed Prime Minister. Allowing Giolitti to become Prime Minister is unexpected, and controversial within the party.
on-arrival: reformist_dissent += 7; maximalist_dissent += 9; communist_dissent += 12; ldr_relation += 5; ul_relation += 10; prime_minister = "Giolitti"; prime_minister_party = ul_name; psi_in_government = 1; leverage += 10
go-to: ministries

This is highly unexpected, and many in the centre and even the Reformist faction of our party is criticizing this decision.

@gasperi
title: Let the PPI have their Prime Minister. This will improve over leverage.
subtitle: Alcide De Gasperi will be appointed Prime Minister. This is unexpected and will cause internal dissent, but may give us a better position in negotiating for other cabinet positions.
on-arrival: reformist_dissent += 5; maximalist_dissent += 5; communist_dissent += 10; ppi_relation += 10; prime_minister = "De Gasperi"; prime_minister_party = "PPI"; psi_in_government = 1; leverage += 5
go-to: ministries

This is highly unexpected, and many in the centre and even the Reformist faction of our party is criticizing this decision.

@liberal_demands
go-to: l_demands_menu

Having met with representatives from the [+ ldr_name +], they have put forward a series of demands we are to either complete or policies we cannot change while in power.
While it seems do have a bit of wiggle room to negotiate, we certainly cannot outright refuse them. What do we do?

**We will have one year to complete the crackdown of violence if given to us.**

Some major ones include: Land Reform, Streetfighting Crackdowns, and Nationalisations,

While some minor ones are: Budget Surplus, Foreign Affairs and Soviet Aid.

@l_demands_menu

- #libdem_major
- #libdem_minor
- @refuse_libdem
- @finalise_libdem
- #drop_libdem_major
- #drop_libdem_minor

@liberal_reform
title: End our violent seizure of land in the South and reject any form of land reform.
subtitle: This will be a major setback if we are to solve the Southern Question anytime soon.
tags: libdem_major
view-if: liberal_reform_accepted = 0
on-arrival: liberal_reform_accepted = 1; liberal_negotiations += 20;
go-to: l_demands_menu

This is a major point of conjecture for the LDR and UL, and secures us significant progress in negotiations.

@liberal_crackdown
title: Begin a crack down of violence in the streets, including the Red Guard.
subtitle: This will force us to rely on the loyalties of government forces.
tags: libdem_major
view-if: liberal_crackdown_accepted = 0
on-arrival: liberal_crackdown_accepted = 1; liberal_negotiations += 20; liberal_crackdown_timer = 12;
go-to: l_demands_menu

This is a major point of conjecture for the LDR and UL, and secures us significant progress in negotiations.

